WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.590 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  PlayStation 4 owners are in the enviable  position of having too many great games  to play this year between neo persona 5  near automata and horizon zero dawn to  name a few  there's always something vying for our  attention thus it's easy to see why some  games simply fall through the cracks  though not for lack of appeal we've  rounded up six games from 2017 that we  think deserve a second chance in the  spotlight now if you'd like to see the  games from other consoles that we  thought you missed out on you can  subscribe to the GameSpot YouTube  channel to check those out but for now  let's kick things off with something a  little different imagine a relatively  quiet town in the American Midwest  populated by anthropomorphic animals  that's possum Springs where night in the  woods takes place and as May a cat human  you experience a readjustment to your  hometown after getting the boot from  college this comes in the form of a  side-scrolling adventure where  conversations character interactions and  minigames propel you through a  complicated and emotional tale of modern  young adulthood  every story beat hidden tidbit line of  dialogue and optional interaction  encourages exploring the beautifully  painted world the game isn't for  everyone  but it hits what it aims for through the  uniquely charming artwork of Scott  Benson and the quirky yet emotive  soundtrack from Alec Aloka the poignant  storytelling is wrapped in elements that  elevated above many games this year  next machina effectively channel's the  classic twin-stick shooter web action of  games like Robotron 2084 and smash TV  but that's because Eugene Jarvis the  creator of those games collaborated  alongside the developer housemarque to  create it  next machina is an intense display of  frenetic action that takes the best from  the work of both creative forces  combining it into an exhilarating  experience of non-stop shooting and  dashing if you want to make things even  more hectic you could jump in with a  friend and play cooperatively next  mocking those exhilarating action makes  it well worth your time but be wary the  game is tough so expect your patience  and reflexes to be tested  [Music]  Tokyo 42 is an isometric action game  that blends elements of top-down  shooters with a little stealth in an  open world its story takes you down the  rabbit hole of joining a secret  organization of assassins but it doesn't  take itself too seriously despite the  brutal nature of the firefights and the  unforgiving and sometimes frustrating  bullet-hell like enemy encounters Tokyo  42 still manages to be cute it's  probably the allure of neon signs and  the adorable cats the game is vibrant  and lively on the surface but creepily  sterile and violent as you fight back  against the unjust authority line light  rebels in its simplicity  you control a bright segment along a  long line riddled with obstacles and  puzzles to the tune of a relaxing and  rich soundtrack that seems pulled from a  Disney Pixar film the complexity of the  challenges increases as you trot along  the different stages so you should  expect to sharpen your logic and timing  skills the further you get with line  light you will activate platforms to  connect segments avoid threatening lines  and sometimes guide them towards your  goal by manipulating the path forward  line light feels like a stress ball and  video game form it's tranquil and  soothing but manages to be engaging with  a minimalist presentation  [Music]  what remains of Edith Finch is a game in  the same vein as dear Esther and gone  home that is to say it's a walking  simulator the premise is that the Finch  family carries a supposed curse that  leads every family member to die in  unexpected and bizarre ways it's got a  very final destination II aspect to it  you play as either Finch as you explore  the huge mysterious family Manor to  unearth how members in your family tree  have passed away this opens the door to  a creative flashbacks where you get to  play the last strange moments leading to  people's deaths the story is powerful  gripping and dark like an episode black  mirror it's full of unexpected twists  and turns and could only be effectively  told through a video game considering  you can beat it in under three hours you  should play it as soon as you can Yakuza  zero and body is what made his  predecessors so endearing for fans of  the series effectively combining its  melodrama and seriousness with a dose of  offbeat absurdist comedy while these  extremes don't seem like they'd work  together the game handles a combo with  ease the result is an entertaining  experience that pulls you in with its  involved narrative makes you break into  laughter with its outlandish Schumer and  immerses you in its authentic take on  Japanese culture it's also a prequel to  the entire series so if you've never  tried a Yakuza game before you owe it to  yourself to play Yakuza zero the six  games you've just seen represent a  fraction of the interesting titles that  have hit the ps4 this year you can head  over to GameSpot calm for a more  extensive look at the ps4's hidden gems  and subscribe to our YouTube channel for  more videos like these covering switch  Xbox one and PC and if you think there's  a game that deserves a bit of extra love  throw it in the comments below

